    <article class="docs-body">
      <h1>Documentation</h1>
      <p>
        Bird is a local-first vector editor. The document is kept in your browser
        (localStorage, then IndexedDB) and is never sent anywhere, unless you
        deliberately use the AI workshop with your own OpenRouter key.
      </p>

      <h2 id="start">Getting started</h2>
      <h3>Standalone web version</h3>
      <p>Open <code>index.html</code> in a modern browser. No server is required for editing, but serving the folder over HTTP is recommended (PDF Web Workers, service worker, clipboard).</p>
      <pre class="code"><code>{`npx serve bird
# http://localhost:3000`}</code></pre>

      <h3>Astro version</h3>
      <pre class="code"><code>{`cd bird/astro
npm install
npm run dev        # http://localhost:4321
npm run build      # output in dist/
npm start          # standalone Node server`}</code></pre>

      <h3>Desktop app</h3>
      <p>
        The Tauri shell embeds exactly the same engine, plus a system menu that
        carries the shortcuts, and native disk access (open, save, export without
        going through the Downloads folder).
      </p>
      <pre class="code"><code>{`cd bird/desktop
npm install
npm run dev      # native window in development
npm run build    # installer for the current OS
# macOS: .dmg · Windows: .msi/.exe · Linux: .deb/.AppImage/.rpm`}</code></pre>

      <h2 id="shortcuts">Shortcuts</h2>
      <p>
        Shortcuts follow Illustrator and Figma. <code>Ctrl</code> becomes <code>⌘</code> on
        macOS. In the desktop app they are carried by the <b>system menu</b> (File,
        Edit, Object, View), so they keep working even when the artboard does not
        have focus. <kbd>F1</kbd> shows the full list, generated from the command
        registry.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>V</code> / <code>P</code> / <code>N</code> / <code>B</code> / <code>I</code></td><td>Select / Pen / Pencil / Brush / Eyedropper</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>R</code> / <code>E</code> / <code>L</code> / <code>T</code></td><td>Rectangle / Ellipse / Line / Text</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>H</code> or <code>Space</code></td><td>Hand (pan the view)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Ctrl+N</code> / <code>Ctrl+O</code> / <code>Ctrl+S</code> / <code>Ctrl+Shift+S</code></td><td>New / Open / Save / Save as</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Ctrl+Shift+P</code> / <code>Ctrl+E</code> / <code>Ctrl+P</code></td><td>Import a file / Export / Print</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Ctrl+Z</code> / <code>Ctrl+Shift+Z</code> / <code>Ctrl+Y</code></td><td>Undo / Redo</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Ctrl+C</code> / <code>Ctrl+X</code> / <code>Ctrl+V</code> / <code>Ctrl+Shift+V</code></td><td>Copy / Cut / Paste / Paste in place</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Ctrl+D</code> / <code>Ctrl+A</code> / <code>Ctrl+Shift+A</code></td><td>Duplicate / Select all / Deselect all</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Ctrl+G</code> / <code>Ctrl+Shift+G</code></td><td>Group / Ungroup</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Ctrl+]</code> / <code>Ctrl+[</code> / <code>Ctrl+Shift+]</code> / <code>Ctrl+Shift+[</code></td><td>Forward / Backward / Bring to front / Send to back</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Ctrl+2</code> / <code>Ctrl+Alt+2</code> / <code>Ctrl+3</code> / <code>Ctrl+Alt+3</code></td><td>Lock / Unlock all / Hide / Show all</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Ctrl+Shift+O</code> / <code>Ctrl+Shift+.</code> / <code>Ctrl+Shift+,</code></td><td>Outline stroke / Larger body / Smaller body</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Ctrl+0</code> / <code>Ctrl+1</code> / <code>Ctrl+=</code> / <code>Ctrl+-</code></td><td>Fit to screen / 100 % / Zoom in / Zoom out</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Ctrl+R</code> / <code>Ctrl+'</code> / <code>Ctrl+;</code></td><td>Rulers / Grid / Snapping</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Ctrl+Page↑</code> / <code>Ctrl+Page↓</code></td><td>Previous / next artboard</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>F1</code> / <code>F11</code> / <code>Ctrl+K</code> / <code>Ctrl+,</code></td><td>Shortcuts / Full screen / Plug-ins / Preferences</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Del</code></td><td>Delete the selection</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Arrows</code> / <code>Shift+Arrows</code></td><td>Move by 1 px / 10 px</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Esc</code> / <code>Enter</code></td><td>Finish or cancel a path</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 id="import">Import</h2>
      <p>Detection is based on the <b>binary signature</b>, not just the extension. A <code>.ai</code> file starting with <code>%PDF-</code> is treated as a PDF; a PostScript <code>.ai</code> goes through the EPS interpreter.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>SVG / SVGZ</b>: scripts, <code>foreignObject</code>, <code>on*</code> handlers and external references are stripped, then imported into Fabric.</li>
        <li><b>PDF</b>: pdf.js rebuilds paths, clips, colours (RGB, grey, CMYK), opacities, images and text. A multi-page PDF offers a chooser: first page, a chosen page, or <b>one artboard per page</b>.</li>
        <li><b>Adobe Illustrator</b>: modern files are PDFs; older ones are PostScript.</li>
        <li><b>EPS</b>: Adobe Illustrator level 2 PostScript interpreter (moveto, lineto, curveto, v, y, arcs, clips, colours, gsave/grestore).</li>
        <li><b>Images</b>: PNG, JPG, WebP, GIF, BMP, AVIF. <b>Video</b>: first frame.</li>
        <li><b>Clipboard</b>: paste an SVG or an image straight onto the artboard.</li>
        <li><b>Projects</b>: <code>.plume</code>, <code>.bird</code> or raw Fabric JSON (automatic migration).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="export">Export</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><b>SVG</b>: whole document or selection, background artboard / transparent / white.</li>
        <li><b>Vector PDF</b>: SVG → svg2pdf → jsPDF. Compatible with Illustrator and printers.</li>
        <li><b>PNG / JPG / WEBP</b>: 1× to 4× scale, transparency (PNG/WEBP) or white background (JPG).</li>
        <li><b>PDF/X-1a:2001</b>: CMYK only, gradients as real shadings, text as outlines, declared ICC profile and bleed box. This is the file to send to a printer.</li>
        <li><b>Multi-artboard PDF</b>: one PDF, one page per artboard, each at its own format.</li>
        <li><b>Copy</b>: SVG or image to the system clipboard.</li>
        <li><b>Project</b>: <code>.plume</code> v3 (artboards, layers, guides, grid, Fabric objects).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="limits">Known limits</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>PDF: broken fonts or fonts without a Unicode table cannot be rebuilt as editable text.</li>
        <li>Screen PDF: non-standard fonts are replaced by Helvetica, Times or Courier. PDF/X works around this by outlining the text.</li>
        <li>The bundled ICC profile (“Bird CMYK”) is structurally valid but generic: load your printer's profile for strict compliance.</li>
        <li>Scripts and active content in imported SVG files are removed for safety.</li>
        <li>The <code>.ai</code> format is never written: use the vector PDF, which Illustrator opens natively.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="api">JavaScript API</h2>
      <p>The application exposes <code>window.Bird</code> after initialisation:</p>
      <pre class="code"><code>{`Bird.version
Bird.document.new()
Bird.document.payload()
Bird.document.load(payload)
Bird.document.open(file)          // File API, all formats
Bird.io.importSVG(svgText)
Bird.io.importPDF(arrayBuffer)   // { svg, width, height, pages }
Bird.io.importEPS(arrayBuffer)   // { group, parsed }
Bird.export({
  format: 'svg' | 'pdf' | 'png' | 'jpg' | 'webp',
  scope: 'document' | 'selection',
  background: 'artboard' | 'transparent' | 'white',
  scale: 1 | 2 | 3 | 4,
  returnBlob: true
})
Bird.exportSVG({ background: 'transparent' })
Bird.zoom.selection()
Bird.selection.lock()
Bird.selection.hide()`}</code></pre>

      <h2 id="astro">Astro build</h2>
      <p>
        <code>bird/astro</code> is a real Astro 7 project with the Node adapter.
        The editor DOM <b>and the script load order</b> are generated from
        <code>../index.html</code> (a single source) by <code>scripts/sync-engine.mjs</code>,
        which also copies <code>js/</code>, <code>css/</code>, <code>icons/</code> and
        <code>plugins/</code> into <code>public/assets/bird/</code>. Adding a file to the
        engine therefore needs no change to <code>editor.astro</code>.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>/</code>: marketing page.</li>
        <li><code>/editor</code>: the full editor.</li>
        <li><code>/docs</code>: this documentation.</li>
        <li><code>/api/health</code>: service status.</li>
        <li><code>/api/ai</code>: optional OpenRouter proxy (<code>OPENROUTER_API_KEY</code> or the <code>x-openrouter-key</code> header).</li>
        <li><code>/sitemap.xml</code>: dynamic sitemap.</li>
      </ul>
      <pre class="code"><code>{`# Development
cd bird/astro
npm run dev

# Production
npm run build
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-... npm start`}</code></pre>

      <h2 id="rust">Rust build</h2>
      <p>
        <code>bird/rust</code> is a Cargo workspace with no heavy dependency in the
        core: Bird document model, SVG/PDF/EPS export, inspection and validation of
        <code>.plume</code> files, local server for the editor.
      </p>
      <pre class="code"><code>{`cd bird/rust

# Windows: detects MSVC or MinGW-W64, tests and builds the CLI
./scripts/build.ps1

# Unix
./scripts/build.sh

# Usage
cargo run -p bird-cli -- inspect examples/sample.plume
cargo run -p bird-cli -- to-svg examples/sample.plume out.svg
cargo run -p bird-cli -- to-pdf examples/sample.plume out.pdf
cargo run -p bird-cli -- to-eps examples/sample.plume out.eps
cargo run -p bird-cli -- serve .. --port 8787`}</code></pre>
      <p class="muted">See <code>bird/rust/README.md</code> for the command grammar and the supported conversions.</p>

      <h2 id="desktop">Desktop app</h2>
      <p>
        <code>bird/desktop</code> is a Tauri 2 shell: the web engine is embedded as is,
        with no rewrite. What the shell adds:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>System menu</b> (File, Edit, Object, View, Window, Help) carrying every
          Adobe/Figma shortcut — they stay active even when the artboard does not
          have focus;</li>
        <li><b>Native files</b>: <code>Ctrl+S</code> writes to the current path,
          <code>Ctrl+Shift+S</code> opens the dialog, SVG/PDF/PDF-X/PNG exports go
          out in one binary call;</li>
        <li><b>Application window</b> whose title reflects the open file, full screen
          (F11), <code>.plume</code> / <code>.bird</code> file associations.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The menu holds no logic: it emits <code>bird://command</code> with an identifier,
        and the engine runs the matching command through <code>BirdCommands</code>. The
        number of shortcuts actually taken by the menu is reported back to the web at
        startup (<code>app_info</code>): if a platform refuses an accelerator, the webview
        keyboard takes over.
      </p>
      <pre class="code"><code>{`# Build (one machine per OS — the CI matrix produces all three)
cd bird/desktop
npx tauri icon ../icons/icon-512.png
npm run build

# Windows + MinGW-W64 (without Visual Studio)
$env:RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN = 'stable-x86_64-pc-windows-gnu'
$env:CARGO_TARGET_X86_64_PC_WINDOWS_GNU_LINKER = '<mingw>/bin/gcc.exe'
npx tauri build --target x86_64-pc-windows-gnu`}</code></pre>
      <p class="muted">
        The macOS (.dmg) and Linux (.deb/.AppImage/.rpm) installers cannot be produced
        from Windows: <code>.github/workflows/desktop.yml</code> builds them on the
        matching runners, as downloadable artifacts and as a GitHub release on a
        <code>v*</code> tag.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>